<!--
NODrugPool.DES - Defines systemic NO drug distribution in HumMod

Overview
This file models the **systemic distribution and clearance** of Nitric Oxide (NO) in the body.
It calculates drug concentration, conversion between units, and overall mass balance, incorporating inputs from **IV infusion, lung absorption, and sublingual administration**.

Key Functions
1. Drug Concentration Calculation: Converts NO mass into relevant physiological units (pg/mL, nMol).
2. Volume of Distribution (`VolDist`): Defines the theoretical volume needed to distribute NO evenly.
3. Mass Balance Equations: Integrates NO gain from different sources (`NODrugIV`, `NODrugLung`, `NODrugSL`) and removes it via `NitricOxideClearance`.

How to Replicate for Another Drug
1. Change `<name> NODrugPool </name>` to `<name> NewDrugPool </name>`.
2. Adjust MW (Molecular Weight) for the new drug.
3. Modify VolDist (Volume of Distribution) based on pharmacokinetic data.
4. Ensure that Gain and Loss account for the new drug’s administration routes and clearance.
5. Link this file in the main drug model (`NODrug.DES`).

-->

HumMod / NODrugPool

Created : 10-Apr-05
Last Modified : 26-Jan-10
Author : Tom Coleman
Copyright : 2006-2010
By : University Of Mississippi Medical Center
Schema : DES V1.0

<structure><name> NODrugPool </name> <!-- Defines systemic NO distribution and clearance -->

<variables>

  <!-- conversion variables -->
<var>
  <name> [NODrug] </name> <!-- NO concentration in systemic circulation -->
</var>
<var>
  <name> [NODrug(pG/mL)] </name> <!-- NO concentration converted to pg/mL -->
</var>
<var>
  <name> [NODrug(nMol)] </name> <!-- NO concentration in nanomolar units -->
</var>
<constant><name> MW </name><val> 30.01 </val></constant> <!-- Molecular Weight of NO (g/mol) -->
<parm>
  <name> VolDist </name> <!-- Theoretical volume needed to evenly distribute NO -->
  <val> 50 </val> <!-- L/kg -->
</parm>



<!-- derv variables -->
<var>
  <name> Gain </name> <!-- Total NO entering circulation -->
</var>
<var>
  <name> Loss </name> <!-- Total NO leaving circulation -->
</var>

<!-- new NTG to NO conversion variables -->
<var>
  <name> NTGmass_uG </name> <!-- Nitroglycerin coming in in mcg-->
</var>
<var>
  <name> NTGtoNitrite_uG </name> <!-- NTG made into nitrite during conversion cascade-->
</var>
<var>
  <name> NO2toNO_Efficiency </name> <!-- factors determining nitrie conversion to NO -->
</var>
<var>
  <name> NOfromNTG </name><val> 0 </val> <!-- NO produced from NTG, in mcg -->
</var>
<var>
  <name> pH </name> <!-- a part of the -->
</var>
<var>
  <name> PO2 </name>
</var>

<var>
  <name> HbFactor </name>
</var>
<var>
  <name> O2Factor </name>
</var>
<var>
  <name> NOtoNitrate_k </name>
</var>
<var>
  <name> NOtoNitrate </name>
</var>

</variables>

<equations>

<diffeq>
  <name> Mass </name> <!-- Defines NO mass balance in the system -->
  <integralname> Mass </integralname>
  <initialval> 0.0 </initialval> <!-- Starts with no NO present -->
  <dervname> Change </dervname>
  <errorlim> 0.00025 </errorlim> <!-- Controls numerical error tolerance -->
</diffeq>
<!-- Units: micrograms -->

</equations>

<definitions>

<block><name> Init </name>

  <def><name> O2Factor </name><val> 0 </val></def>
  <def><name> HbFactor </name><val> 0 </val></def>

  <def><name> NTGmass_uG </name><val> 0 </val></def>
  <def><name> PO2 </name><val> 0 </val></def>

  <def><name> pH </name><val> 0 </val></def>
  <def><name> NOfromNTG </name><val> 0 </val></def>

  <def><name> NO2toNO_Efficiency </name><val> 0 </val></def>
  <def><name> NTGtoNitrite_uG </name><val> 0 </val></def>

</block>


<block><name> Parms </name>

  <def><name> pH </name><val> BloodPhValues.VeinsPh </val></def>
  <def><name> PO2 </name><val> O2Veins.[O2] </val></def> <!-- undefined; remains zero -->
  <def><name> NTGmass_uG </name><val> NTGPool.NTGIntake </val></def>

</block>

<block><name> NTGtoNO_Pathway </name>

<call> NODrugPool.Init </call>
<call> NODrugPool.Parms </call>

<!-- pool just for sake of conversion, activity process highlighted in systemic veins section 
  <call> O2Veins.CalcConc </call>
  <call> NTGPool.Dervs </call> -->

  <!-- Step 2: ALDH2 activity approximation: ~35% NTG becomes nitrite, no dynamic ALDH2 modulators to use here? -->
  <def><name> NTGtoNitrite_uG </name>
    <val> NTGmass_uG * 0.35 </val>
  </def>
  <!-- citation: Beretta M, Gorren AC, Wenzl MV, Weis R, Russwurm M, Koesling D, Schmidt K, Mayer B. Characterization of the East Asian variant of aldehyde dehydrogenase-2: bioactivation of nitroglycerin and effects of Alda-1. J Biol Chem. 2010 Jan 8;285(2):943-52. doi: 10.1074/jbc.M109.014548. Epub 2009 Nov 11. PMID: 19906643; PMCID: PMC2801295.-->
<!-- ADLH2's activator common in East Asians (ALDA-1) also significant in Asian Flush phenomenon -->
<!-- clarify ALDH2 function in East Asians - promotes/doesn't promote ALDH2 production? -->


  <!-- Step 3: Nitrite to NO conversion efficiency (modulated by O2 and pH to make more dynamic) -->
  <def><name> NO2toNO_Efficiency </name>
    <val>
      0.10
      + 0.15 * ( 1 - PO2 / 100 )
      + 0.10 * ( 9 - pH )
    </val>
  </def>

  <!-- 0.10: kept as baseline efficiency (10%) for NO formation from nitrite  under normal conditions.
   2nd line: as venous PO₂ decreases (i.e., hypoxia), this term increases, ex. when PO2 = 100 mmHg: → this term = 0
   3rd line: As blood becomes more acidic (pH < 7.37), this term increases, ex. at 7.37, this term = 0. 7.37 is used as baseline pH in Hummod.
  -->
  <!-- Step 4: Final NO yield -->
  <def><name> NOfromNTG </name>
    <val> NTGtoNitrite_uG * NO2toNO_Efficiency </val>
  </def>

</block>

<block><name> CalcConc </name> 
<def>
  <name> [NODrug] </name>
  <val> Mass / VolDist </val> <!-- Computes systemic NO concentration -->
</def>
<!-- mcg/L -->
<def>
  <name> [NODrug(nMol)] </name>
  <val> 1000 * [NODrug] / MW </val> <!-- Converts NO concentration to nanomolar from microgram -->
</def>

</block>

<block><name> Dervs </name> <!-- Defines how NO is added or removed from circulation -->

<call> NODrugLiver.Dervs </call> 
<call> PO2Artys.Calc </call>
<call> HgbConc.Calc </call>

<!-- Define NO to Nitrate -->
<def><name> O2Factor </name>
  <val> 1.0 + ( PO2Artys.Pressure / 100 ) </val>
</def>
<def><name> HbFactor </name>
  <val> 1.0 + ( HgbConc.[Total] / 15 ) </val>
</def>
<def><name> NOtoNitrate_k </name>
  <val> 0.02 * O2Factor * HbFactor </val>
</def>
<def><name> NOtoNitrate </name>
  <val> Mass * NOtoNitrate_k </val>
</def>


<def>
  <name> Gain </name>
  <val> NOfromNTG * 1000 </val> <!-- Total NO entering the pool -->
</def>
<def>
  <name> Loss </name>
  <val>  NOtoNitrate + NODrugLiver.PortalMass </val> <!-- Total NO leaving circulation (clearance or liver) -->
</def>
<def>
  <name> Change </name>
  <val> Gain - Loss </val> <!-- Net NO change in the body -->
</def>

</block>

</definitions>
</structure>

End

<!-- source for conversion: 
  Parmar, V., Orabi, E. A., English, A. M., & Peslherbe, G. H. (2024). Modeling predicts facile release of nitrite but not nitric oxide from the thionitrate CH3SNO2 with relevance to nitroglycerin bioactivation. Scientific Reports, 14(1), Article 31552. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-80230-7
 -->
